;;; M1a — two `Fact` arguments in one hypothesis slot.
;;;
;;; `mixed-type-hypothesis.ein` puts an int and a symbol in the same slot of
;;; two candidates. This is the *other* half of the same ordering question,
;;; produced by the S1a.6.6 fuzzer: two candidates whose argument is a
;;; nested fact, compared element-wise by the commitment search's content
;;; order. `Value` is totally ordered (Int < Sym < Fact, and a nested fact by
;;; its own content), so the run answers `Solution`.
;;;
;;; **It needs no mixed slot types.** One hrule whose `:assert` head is a
;;; `(not …)` is enough — the hypothesis's argument is then a nested fact, and
;;; two of them reach the layer-1 sort of `alive`. That is an ordinary
;;; puzzle idiom, so the case is wider than its first fixture suggested.
;;; What stays true is the *scope* claim: blind generation still cannot get
;;; here (its candidates are built from `kb.names()`, all symbols), so an
;;; `(hrule …)` remains necessary.

(is-a o1 T)
(is-a o2 T)

(hrule hyp-0 ()
  :match  (is-a ?v0 T)
  :assert (not (r0 ?v0 o2)))
